## Summary

In the additive noise problem, the `EM` and `RKMil` algorithms do not reach the sample error in the latest run. On the scalar wave SDE, `EM` and `RKMil` reach the sample error about as cheaply as the higher order methods, while on the linear scalar noise problem they need more time than the Rössler methods to reach it. The dashed "Sample Error: 1000" line comes from `DiffEqDevTools.get_sample_errors`, which currently divides by $\sqrt{N}$ twice, so it sits about $\sqrt{1000}$ below $E_S = \sqrt{V/N}$; treat comparisons against that line as provisional until the library fix lands.

